2186542120 #edb/ref 1071 "A backpack costs $$64$ before tax. If a $7.75%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542120 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16908ce6-e741-598e-9537-76866b0601d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542120 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $7.75%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.75%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+7.75%=107.75%=1.0775\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0775:$\n\n$$\n64\\times 1.0775=68.96.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the backpack after tax is $$68.96.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1071 "$(x-3y)({x}^{2}+4y)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c104695-c936-5d0f-b4ab-74c8ede24e1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542135 #edb/ref 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)({x}^{2}+4y) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+4y)-3y({x}^{2}+4y) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+4xy-3{x}^{2}y-12{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1071 "$3+\\frac{3}{2}x-1-\\frac{7}{4}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49e631ed-e489-57e1-8328-8ad962002fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542142 #edb/ref 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+\\frac{3x}{2}-1-\\frac{7x}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{7x}{4})+(3-1) & = \\\\ (\\frac{6}{4}-\\frac{7}{4})x+(3-1) & = \\\\ (\\frac{6-7}{4})x+2 & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{3(2x-4)}{4(5-x)}$ for $x=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5270a65a-9011-5c4a-8ec5-b8423546a5ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542147 #edb/ref 1074 "We replace $x$ with $6$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(2x-4)}{4(5-x)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(2(6)-4)}{4(5-6)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(8)}{4(-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{24}{-4} & = \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{1}{15}=-\\frac{m}{3},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "651d48b6-dcdd-5bc2-87b4-a66ffecf04ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542151 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nThen, we remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{3} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{m}{3}) & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is being divided by $3.$ To isolate $m$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{m}{3} & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ \\frac{3m}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}m}{\\cancel{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1071 "The additive inverse of $24$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69122235-d824-5d29-8715-1c0104966b58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542154 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the additive inverse of the number $24$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,(24)=-24.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n24+(-24)=24-24=0\\,✓\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $(-32)^{0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c864496-70bc-5f2d-82d0-559a3bc9072f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542158 #edb/ref 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-32)^{0}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2d3fc4b568492c8435f72c28010c21b0c0746e2abe46922fdb942eaba5cf8b05.png)\n\nWhat $x:y$ ratio is shown in the graph above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ca180bb-426b-5acf-99a3-c4512087f7c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542159 #edb/ref 1074 "We see that the point $(7,5)$ lies on the graph. Let's write the ratio $7:5$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can not simplify this fraction further. Hence, we can say that the graph shows the ratio $7:5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1071 "If $p=-1,$ then $|4p+1{|}^{3}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9169a9eb-58fd-54ef-9fd7-4f2515dd156b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542170 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute $p=-1$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4p+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |4(-1)+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-4+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-3{|}^{3} & = \\\\ (3)^{3} & = \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $0<x\\le 7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eee859cf-032a-5a34-a81e-4f392e60f1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542222 #edb/ref 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality \n\n$$\n0\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,7\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $0$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $(\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $7$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $]\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding interval is\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,7].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "016be58b-34c7-56e0-bb25-ce68f3db4b2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05c3145b-6657-521b-9f88-78380cc745b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a65f657-f98a-5a67-89c2-bc016942eb55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11d78059-663a-593e-a69e-1ea1a37547dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13dd978b-1f46-5e2b-91cd-11bd806fc55d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "238578fd-c90c-54cc-b558-55ce62403117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542247 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542247 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31915826-2175-53bc-9331-33006aaf0069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542247 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138753 #